<question>What are the typical symptoms and risk factors of GERD?</question>
[SYNTH_REPLY] GERD is a condition where stomach contents flow back into the esophagus or throat, causing symptoms or complications [18: "reflux of stomach contents into the esophagus or oral cavity resulting in symptoms or complications"]. The typical symptoms are heartburn and the backup of stomach contents into the throat [18: "typical symptoms of GERD are heartburn and regurgitation of gastric contents into the oropharynx"]. In children, symptoms can be varied and nonspecific, meaning they are not clear-cut and require careful evaluation [19: "clinical manifestations of GERD in young children are varied and nonspecific prompting the necessity for careful diagnostic evaluation"]. In children specifically, risk factors include a hiatal hernia, problems with the lining of the esophagus that should protect it, and trouble with how the esophagus and stomach move and clear contents [19: "defects in esophageal mucosal defense, impaired esophageal and gastric motility and clearance, as well as anatomical defects of the lower esophageal reflux barrier such as hiatal hernia"]. The condition can lead to more serious problems over time, including inflammation of the esophagus, narrowing of the esophagus, and a precancerous change called Barrett esophagus [18: "erosive esophagitis, esophageal strictures, and Barrett esophagus, a precursor to esophageal adenocarcinoma"].
