[SYNTH_REPLY] The typical symptoms of GERD are heartburn and regurgitation of stomach contents into the throat or mouth [18: "The typical symptoms of GERD are heartburn and regurgitation of gastric contents into the oropharynx"]. GERD happens when stomach contents flow back into the esophagus or mouth, and it can affect a person's quality of life [18: "results from the reflux of stomach contents into the esophagus or oral cavity resulting in symptoms or complications"][18: "GERD affects quality of life"]. If left untreated, it can lead to complications including inflammation of the esophagus, narrowing of the esophagus, and a condition called Barrett esophagus, which can be a precursor to esophageal cancer [18: "may cause erosive esophagitis, esophageal strictures, and Barrett esophagus, a precursor to esophageal adenocarcinoma"]. In children, the symptoms can be varied and nonspecific, making diagnosis more challenging [19: "The clinical manifestations of GERD in young children are varied and nonspecific prompting the necessity for careful diagnostic evaluation"]. Several factors can contribute to GERD, including abnormal relaxing of the valve between the esophagus and stomach, weakness in the esophagus's protective lining, problems with how the esophagus and stomach move and clear food, and anatomical defects such as a hiatal hernia [19: "abnormal transient relaxations of the lower esophageal sphincter"][19: "defects in esophageal mucosal defense, impaired esophageal and gastric motility and clearance, as well as anatomical defects of the lower esophageal reflux barrier such as hiatal hernia"]. Symptoms of GERD are among the most commonly reported to primary care providers [20: "Symptoms potentially attributable to gastroesophageal reflux disease are among those most commonly reported to primary care providers in the outpatient setting"]. The condition is also known by the name Gastroesophageal Reflux [3], and a related medical record titled Gastroesophageal reflux (GERD) exists in the MedGen database [2]. Several research publications are listed under the title Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease and carry the identifiers PMID 29132520 [5], PMID 33351044 [8], PMID 36170502 [11], PMID 37034973 [14], and PMID 37516506 [17]. A publication titled Gastroesophageal reflux disease in children: What's new right now? is also available [12]. Clinical trials studying treatments for GERD include Famotidine in Subjects With Non-erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease [4], Efficacy of Pantoprazole in Patients Older Than 18 Years Who Have Symptoms of Acid Reflux (Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease) (BY1023/M3-339) [7], Natural History of Gastroesophageal Reflux (GER) in Children and Adolescents [10], On Demand Treatment of Reflux Disease [13], and Treatment Of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease By Endoscopic Fundoplication, A Placebo-Controlled Study [16].
